import {v7EditorialGuides} from './expansion-v7-editorial-guides.js?v=5601ad9fd5efe681';
// Original method guides. These explain model choices, not independent reviews of
// every numerical example. Problem-specific worked traces and deep notes are bound
// to the released title, prompt and version in editorial-catalog.js.
const guide=(title,takeaway,steps,mistake,reason,check,design,assumptions)=>({title,takeaway,steps,mistake,reason,check,design,assumptions});
export const editorialGuides={
 ...v7EditorialGuides,
 node:guide('Audit the node by branch currents','A voltage answer becomes convincing when every connected branch balances.',[
 'Choose ground and a leaving-current convention. A branch from node v to a known source Vs contributes (v − Vs)/R; an injected source contributes a negative leaving current.',
 'Collect the conductance terms before inserting numbers. This exposes parallel branches and the sign of a controlled injection.',
 'After solving, reconstruct each branch current from the original topology. The signed sum must be zero, even when a branch flows opposite the assumed direction.'
 ],'Treat every connected resistor as a series chain.','Series elements share the same current only when their junction has no other branch. A loaded divider needs the load in KCL.','Compare the reduced-network result with the unreduced branch-current residual.','Reducing resistance lowers output impedance but consumes more current. A buffer reduces loading while introducing its own offset, input range and power requirements.','Independent sources are ideal only where stated; retain dependent sources and all physical load branches.'),
 supernode:guide('Recover the internal source current afterwards','A floating voltage source constrains a voltage difference, not either node’s voltage to ground.',[
 'Draw a boundary enclosing both source terminals. Sum only currents that cross that boundary; the internal ideal-source current cancels.',
 'Write the source voltage difference with its stated polarity as a second equation, then solve the two node voltages together.',
 'Return to KCL at just one terminal to recover the source current. Check the other terminal independently using that same signed current.'
 ],'Set the positive terminal directly to the source voltage.','The negative terminal is not necessarily ground. Omitting its unknown voltage removes a needed degree of freedom.','Use both individual-node KCL equations to check a solution obtained from the supernode.','Floating supplies and level shifts require a reference convention. Actual source current or common-mode limits can invalidate an otherwise valid ideal-node solution.','An ideal floating voltage source supports whatever internal current KCL requires; no current limit is implied.'),
 mesh:guide('Cross-check with node voltages','Shared resistors respond to the difference of mesh currents.',[
 'Assign a direction to each mesh current and write its shared-branch current as the algebraic difference of the two mesh currents.',
 'Solve the coupled loop equations without replacing the shared resistor by two independent elements.',
 'Reconstruct a node voltage from either loop and use branch KCL at the shared junction as a separate residual check.'
 ],'Add the two mesh currents through a shared branch.','With consistently oriented meshes, their directions oppose on the common resistor. The sign follows the drawn arrows.','An independent branch-current sum must agree with the mesh-current difference.','Mesh analysis is efficient for a few planar loops; node analysis often scales better when many branches meet a small number of nodes.','Preserve source polarity while traversing a loop and distinguish a mesh variable from a physical branch current.'),
 port:guide('Use an explicit test source at the port','A terminal equivalent must preserve both open-circuit voltage and incremental resistance.',[
 'Define the port voltage and current direction. Find the open-circuit voltage from the original active network.',
 'For resistance, deactivate independent voltage sources to shorts and independent current sources to opens. Keep every controlled source active and apply a test voltage or current.',
 'Compute Vtest/Itest under the declared entering-current convention. Where applicable, verify the Norton relation Vth = In Rth.'
 ],'Turn off a dependent source or discard a negative resistance.','A controlled source remains part of the port response. An active network can return power and have negative incremental resistance.','Substitute a second test-source magnitude: a linear port should give the same resistance ratio.','An equivalent circuit simplifies repeated load calculations. Its linear range, source compliance and allowed operating point still limit a real active port.','The port model applies only to the stated network and the same terminal orientation; source-deactivation rules apply to independent sources.'),
 superposition:guide('Add signed responses, then calculate power','Linear circuit responses superpose; their powers usually do not.',[
 'Retain one independent source at a time and deactivate the other independent sources with the correct short/open replacement.',
 'Solve each voltage or current contribution using the same reference direction. Negative contributions are allowed.',
 'Add the signed responses. If power is requested, use the combined voltage/current in the original circuit rather than adding separately computed source-response powers.'
 ],'Open an inactive ideal voltage source.','A zero-volt ideal source is a short. Opening it changes the topology and the resistance seen by the active source.','Check that the summed response satisfies the complete original KCL or KVL equation.','Superposition helps isolate disturbance sensitivity. It requires a linear model; device clipping or a change of transistor region breaks the same-model sum.','Only independent excitations are separated. Dependent sources remain active in every partial circuit.'),
 power:guide('Check the energy direction and feasible load','A positive power number needs an explicit voltage-current reference.',[
 'Use the passive sign convention: positive absorbed power is voltage times current entering the marked positive terminal.',
 'For a variable load, express load power as a function of that allowed load variable before optimizing. A resistor-only constraint differs from arbitrary complex matching.',
 'Check the candidate against the original load limits and sum supplied and absorbed real power. Use RMS quantities for average sinusoidal resistor power.'
 ],'Equate maximum power transfer with maximum efficiency.','Matching can intentionally dissipate significant power in the source resistance. It optimizes one load-power objective, not all design objectives.','Check the objective at nearby load values and at the allowed boundary, then confirm total power balance.','Power matching suits some signal interfaces; efficient power delivery generally favors lower source loss. Thermal ratings and source limits can dominate the theoretical optimum.','A passive load cannot choose a negative resistance, and a resistor-only load cannot cancel source reactance.'),
 phasor:guide('Rebuild the result in rectangular form','Phase is information; magnitudes alone cannot enforce AC circuit laws.',[
 'Convert every impedance at the stated angular frequency. Inductor impedance is +jωL and capacitor impedance is −j/(ωC).',
 'Apply KCL or KVL using complex quantities. Add real and imaginary components before finding a magnitude or phase.',
 'Recover average real power from resistor dissipation or Re(VI*) and compare with the source real power under the same RMS convention.'
 ],'Add current magnitudes or use apparent power as heat.','Out-of-phase currents add as vectors. Only the real part of complex power is average delivered energy per second.','For a passive inductive input, current lags voltage; a capacitive input leads. Confirm the sign from the computed imaginary current.','Reactive currents increase RMS loading without equal real load power. Compensation can reduce source current but adds frequency sensitivity and component stress.','Use sinusoidal steady state and one RMS/peak convention throughout; transient initial conditions are excluded from a phasor solution.'),
 transformer:guide('Verify ratios with port power and winding polarity','Transformer current and impedance ratios must satisfy power and the stated dot convention.',[
 'Write the voltage ratio using the named winding order and polarity. Reflect an impedance with the square of that same turns ratio.',
 'When magnetizing inductance is present, add its current at the primary node as a complex branch current, distinct from reflected load current.',
 'For coupled inductors, use signed mutual terms in both winding equations. Check stored energy or real/reactive power rather than comparing magnitudes alone.'
 ],'Scale resistance by only one turns ratio.','Voltage and current scale oppositely, so their ratio scales by the square. Magnetizing current also prevents equating total source current with reflected load current.','Confirm ideal-transformer real-power equality and account separately for any stated series loss, leakage or magnetizing branch.','Turns ratio changes voltage/current levels; magnetizing current, copper loss, core limits and winding capacitance constrain real parts. These exercises include only their named effects.','Dots fix relative polarity, not an unconditional plus sign; use the actual current directions and terminal references.'),
 noise:guide('Budget variance before taking RMS','Independent noise terms add in mean square, not directly in amplitude.',[
 'Refer all noise contributions to the same input or output and integrate over the same stated bandwidth.',
 'Square each RMS contribution, add independent variances and take a square root at the end.',
 'For an allocated remaining budget, subtract used variances from the total variance before solving for the new allowed RMS term.'
 ],'Subtract RMS voltages directly from the budget.','Independent random terms combine through variance. Correlated sources require covariance terms and cannot use the same simple RSS rule.','The combined RMS should exceed each individual independent contribution but remain below their straight amplitude sum.','Lower source resistance can reduce resistor noise while increasing bias power. Bandwidth limiting reduces integrated noise but slows the wanted signal.','Independence and the supplied bandwidth are modeling assumptions, not automatic properties of all circuit disturbances.'),
 line:guide('Follow a separate timeline for each wave','A transmission line cannot be reduced to its final DC divider before waves arrive.',[
 'Launch the first wave using source impedance and characteristic impedance, then mark its one-way travel time.',
 'At each boundary, multiply the arriving wave by that boundary’s signed reflection coefficient. The local voltage change includes incident plus reflected waves.',
 'Propagate the reflected wave back with the appropriate delay and accumulate all prior local voltage steps at the observation point.'
 ],'Apply a reflection coefficient to the total accumulated voltage.','A boundary reflects the newly arriving traveling wave. Re-reflecting the stored level counts old energy again.','Track the direction, sign and arrival time of every term; a node cannot respond to a reflection before its round trip.','Termination reduces reflections at a power cost. Short lumped traces and long distributed lines need different models relative to edge speed.','The stated lossless characteristic impedance and propagation delay replace lumped-capacitor intuition during the specified arrival window.'),
 firstOrder:guide('Solve for the error from the final value','The exponential applies to the initial-to-final gap, including its sign.',[
 'Find the final DC state with the capacitor open and determine its voltage just after switching from continuity, unless the prompt explicitly supplies an impulse/reconfiguration model.',
 'Find the resistance seen by the capacitor in the new topology, deactivating independent sources, then form τ = Rseen C.',
 'Propagate the remaining error: v(t) − vf = [v(0+) − vf] exp(−t/τ). For a threshold, isolate that ratio before taking the natural logarithm.'
 ],'Use the supply voltage as the final capacitor voltage in a loaded network.','The final state follows the full DC network. A load or leakage path can lower the final voltage and also change the time constant.','Check t = 0, t → infinity, the direction of motion and whether the requested threshold lies between the initial and final values.','A larger R can reduce source-current demand but worsen leakage and loading errors. A larger C stores more charge but increases settling time at fixed resistance.','The one-pole formula requires one independent storage state in each interval and the actual post-switch resistance.'),
 charge:guide('Conserve charge on the correct isolated conductors','Charge sharing conserves the stated isolated-node charge; it need not conserve capacitor energy.',[
 'Identify which conductors remain electrically isolated through the event and write signed plate charges relative to the stated voltage references.',
 'Apply charge conservation with the new connection constraints. A floating capacitor’s differential voltage and an absolute node voltage are different quantities.',
 'Compute initial and final stored energy separately. Any drop is dissipated in the stipulated passive transfer path rather than evidence of missing charge.'
 ],'Conserve capacitor energy during passive charge sharing.','Finite equalization current dissipates energy even when the transfer duration is treated as negligible. Grounded or driven conductors may exchange charge with sources.','Reconstruct every plate charge after reconfiguration and verify the conserved conductor totals, including sign.','More reservoir capacitance reduces voltage disturbance for a given transferred charge but costs area and startup charge. Switching sequence and parasitic paths matter in hardware.','Apply continuity only to the storage variable retained by the stated event model; do not impose ground-referenced continuity on every floating plate.'),
 periodic:guide('Solve one cycle as a fixed point','Periodic steady state means the state repeats after the complete sequence, not after one subinterval.',[
 'Write a state update for each interval using its own source, resistance and storage configuration. Retain both the starting value and elapsed interval time.',
 'Compose the updates into one cycle map xnext = ax + b, then solve x = xnext where the map is affine.',
 'Check |a| < 1 for convergence. Recover extrema from their exact event locations and compute mean load current from transferred charge or an integral.'
 ],'Use the average input as though the waveform had no ripple.','Averaging loses the finite interval excursions and can misidentify the requested peak, minimum or threshold time.','Iterate the cycle from a second starting state and confirm convergence toward the same fixed point.','Faster switching or larger reservoirs usually reduce ripple within the model; switching loss, source charge demand and physical transfer time limit that trend.','Each subinterval’s topology and duration matter. A disconnected flying capacitor must not be included in the hold capacitance.'),
 filter:guide('Derive the complex divider before quoting a corner','A filter pole, its DC insertion gain and its absolute signal gain are different quantities.',[
 'Replace the capacitor with 1/(jωC), retain source and load impedances, and derive the output-to-input complex ratio.',
 'Separate the DC gain factor from the frequency-dependent factor. A resistive load can move the pole while also reducing passband amplitude.',
 'Evaluate low/high-frequency limits and the requested phase with a quadrant-aware argument. Cascaded stage gains multiply only when the interstage loading is included or buffered away.'
 ],'Use 1/(2πRC) with an unrelated R or ignore interstage loading.','The storage element sees the complete resistance of the topology. An unbuffered ladder generally has coupled poles rather than two independent copies.','Verify dimensions, limiting gains and the complex node-equation residual at a non-corner frequency.','Increasing R and decreasing C can preserve a pole while reducing source current, but changes noise and load sensitivity. Buffering adds power and finite bandwidth.','The transfer describes linear sinusoidal steady state; capacitor ESR, input impedance and buffers exist only where explicitly specified.'),
 secondOrder:guide('Check both roots and their physical constraints','Multiple energy-storage states require more than a single time constant.',[
 'Write the characteristic polynomial from KCL/KVL or a state matrix, retaining coupling terms between storage elements.',
 'Find both poles or frequency roots and keep the physically allowed roots. Use initial values and derivatives to determine modal coefficients.',
 'For resonance bandwidth, solve the exact half-power condition rather than assuming a narrow-band approximation when Q is small.'
 ],'Replace a coupled two-state network by one RC product.','Interstage currents depend on both state voltages, and a broad resonance need not be symmetric in linear frequency.','Substitute the poles into the characteristic equation and check both initial conditions as well as the long-time limit.','Extra poles can sharpen filtering while adding settling tails, overshoot or phase lag. Damping trades peaking against transient speed.','A second-order approximation is useful only for the elements and idealizations explicitly retained in the question.'),
 integration:guide('Integrate the defined waveform or constitutive law','The exact waveform shape determines charge and energy.',[
 'Start from i = C dv/dt, v = L di/dt or p = vi using the problem’s terminal convention.',
 'Write each time interval’s stated waveform and integrate over that interval. Avoid substituting an average into a nonlinear power product.',
 'Check the resulting units and compare the integral with stored-energy or charge change at the endpoints.'
 ],'Assume a triangular loss pulse whenever two quantities change linearly.','The product of two changing ramps is generally quadratic. A continuing supply can also deliver energy while an inductor discharges.','Differentiate the integrated result or check an independent energy balance, including source work and named losses.','Faster transitions reduce some overlap losses but can increase ringing, EMI and gate-drive demand. The supplied waveform is a reduced model.','Use the exact integration bounds and event sequence; a time-average current and an RMS current answer different questions.'),
 bjtBias:guide('Reduce the base network, then verify junctions','Finite base current changes the surrounding bias network.',[
 'Where possible reduce the external base supply network to its Thévenin equivalent, retaining the emitter return and collector-fed bias branches.',
 'Use IE = (β + 1)IB and IC = βIB in forward active operation. Write base/emitter KVL and any collector-node KCL before solving.',
 'Recover VB, VE and VC. Verify forward bias of the base-emitter junction and the stated collector-base or saturation condition.'
 ],'Set emitter current equal to collector current when finite β is required.','The emitter resistor carries both collector and base current; collector-fed bias paths can also change the collector resistor current.','Reconstruct every supply current from node voltages and confirm the original KCL, then test the assumed region.','Emitter degeneration improves bias stability while costing voltage headroom and gain. A stiffer bias divider reduces loading error but consumes more standing current.','A fixed VBE and β are local exercise parameters. They do not establish a temperature-independent production bias point.'),
 bjtSignal:guide('Obtain the same gain from base-current accounting','Small-signal gain depends on the bias and on what the source actually drives.',[
 'Determine gm from the specified collector current and thermal voltage, then rπ = β/gm under the supplied hybrid-π model.',
 'Reflect emitter loading into the base using β + 1 where that relation applies. Include source attenuation separately from transistor-stage gain.',
 'Use collector/emitter KCL to recover the output sign and all load branches, including ro only where supplied.'
 ],'Apply an unloaded stage gain directly from the source voltage.','Source resistance, bias resistance and an emitter load can attenuate the base signal before the intrinsic gain acts.','Verify the DC operating region separately, then substitute the signal solution into the hybrid-π node equations.','Degeneration and follower stages trade gain for linearity or drive capability. Larger gain can increase Miller input loading and reduce bandwidth.','Coupling capacitors are signal shorts only in the specified frequency range; infinitesimal-signal analysis does not establish large-signal swing.'),
 differential:guide('Use sum and difference currents as independent constraints','The tail constraint fixes the sum while the input law fixes the split.',[
 'Write the exact current-sum constraint, distinguishing emitter tail current from collector-current sum when finite base currents are retained.',
 'Use the given junction or square-law relation to connect input difference to the two currents. Check both currents stay in their physical domains.',
 'Form the requested output subtraction with its exact sign. For a local gain, differentiate at the solved operating point while preserving the current-sum constraint.'
 ],'Use the zero-input small-signal gain at an arbitrary finite differential voltage.','The nonlinear split and its local derivative vary with bias; a chord ratio vout/vin is not generally the derivative.','Confirm both collector/node headroom conditions and check that the current sum remains fixed after perturbing the input.','Degeneration broadens the useful input range but reduces gain and consumes headroom. Tail-source compliance and resistor matching matter in a physical pair.','The supplied matched-device law and tail-source behavior define the model; mismatch and noise are excluded unless named.'),
 bjtSwitch:guide('Compare drive availability with load demand','A load-limited saturated current is different from an unconstrained βIB estimate.',[
 'Find the current demanded by the collector load using the stated saturated voltage and every series LED or supply drop.',
 'Compute base current from its actual driver voltage, base-emitter drop and resistor network. Include current diverted into shunts or clamps.',
 'Check the specified forced-beta or empirical clamp rule, driver budget and resistor dissipation together. If drive is insufficient, do not claim the assumed saturated current is achieved.'
 ],'Send every available driver milliamp through the base.','A bleed resistor or Schottky clamp creates a separate branch. KCL determines how much current actually reaches each terminal.','Close the collector voltage loop and base-node current balance independently.','More drive improves static saturation margin but can increase stored charge and GPIO power. A clamp can redirect drive and speed recovery at a changed static operating point.','A forced-beta rule is a design assumption, not a guaranteed minimum transistor gain or a switching-speed specification.'),
 temperature:guide('Distinguish a supplied temperature law from a universal rule','Temperature sensitivity follows the model’s actual derivatives and feedback.',[
 'Convert temperature to kelvin when using VT = kT/q or an absolute-temperature proportional relation.',
 'Apply the stated device temperature law while holding only the variables the question says are fixed. Re-solve any feedback network rather than changing the device in isolation.',
 'Compare the before/after operating points and verify region or thermal stability conditions at both.'
 ],'Use Celsius directly in an absolute-temperature relation.','A proportional-to-temperature result references absolute zero; a specified voltage slope per degree instead uses a temperature difference.','Check units of the slope and distinguish a local sensitivity from a finite temperature change.','Thermal feedback can reduce or amplify drift. Bias feedback and thermal resistance trades affect accuracy, power and available headroom.','The temperature coefficient is supplied for the exercise. Extrapolating it beyond its stated range is not a device guarantee.'),
 mosBias:guide('Treat operating-region equations as conditional models','An algebraic root is only a candidate until terminal inequalities agree.',[
 'Express VGS, VDS and, when needed, VSB using source-referenced voltages. Do not assume the source is grounded.',
 'Choose a candidate cutoff, triode or saturation equation using the stated k convention. Solve the resulting balance without dropping feedback or load terms.',
 'Substitute every candidate back into the original on-state and region inequalities. Reject roots created by squaring a negative overdrive or extending a branch outside its domain.'
 ],'Use an absolute gate voltage or accept every quadratic root.','Threshold and channel equations depend on gate-to-source and drain-to-source differences; squaring can hide a forbidden overdrive sign.','Evaluate the equation residual and each region inequality separately, including any stated boundary convention.','Source degeneration improves bias stability but reduces gain and headroom. Compliance windows can be as limiting as the nominal current.','Keep the given square-law factor, threshold model and body connection; modern-device accuracy is not implied by this long-channel model.'),
 mosBody:guide('Solve the source node and threshold together','Moving a source can change both VGS and threshold when the body stays fixed.',[
 'Write VSB from the body and source connections. Apply the supplied threshold-versus-VSB law before solving current or headroom.',
 'For a pass high, cascode or follower, relate the threshold to the unknown source voltage rather than inserting the zero-body-bias threshold.',
 'For an incremental gain, retain gmb with its correct controlling voltage. Verify all junction/region domains and the headroom of every stacked device.'
 ],'Tie the body to the source in the algebra when it is physically grounded.','That removes body effect and changes the threshold or small-signal denominator. A fixed body is a separate terminal.','Evaluate the threshold at the solved source voltage and at each requested compliance boundary, not once for all cases.','Body effect reduces pass-high level or follower gain and increases stacked bias requirements. A suitable body connection depends on process isolation and device constraints.','The supplied body law is valid only in its stated domain; intrinsic body-diode conduction is a different mechanism.'),
 mosSignal:guide('Write the controlled current before reducing the gain','The gm source and output resistances must obey the actual node voltages.',[
 'Form gm, gmb and ro from the given bias/model, retaining exact output-slope factors when the problem supplies them.',
 'Write id = gm vgs + gmb vbs + vds/ro with the stated sign. Include load, bias-source resistance and degeneration in node KCL.',
 'Reduce the gain or test resistance from those equations, then compare with an ideal limit such as ro → infinity or zero degeneration.'
 ],'Add a source resistor to the drain load as though it were passive series wiring.','The source motion controls the dependent gm current. Degeneration changes effective transconductance through feedback.','Check the simplified result against the original KCL and the limit where the relevant nonideality vanishes.','Cascoding and degeneration can improve output resistance or linearity at a headroom and gain cost. More resistance is not automatically more available voltage swing.','These are incremental results at the stated bias; transistor capacitances and large-signal clipping are separate checks.'),
 gateDrive:guide('Budget charge, current and conduction by interval','A gate with negligible DC current can still demand substantial switching charge.',[
 'Identify whether the supplied quantity is capacitance, total gate charge, Miller charge or a prescribed time waveform; those define different calculations.',
 'Use the driver’s actual voltage and resistance during the relevant interval. Include leakage, refresh periods or clamp paths where stated.',
 'Compute charge per event, average source power or instantaneous loss from the stated waveform, then verify the limiting gate voltage or current.'
 ],'Use one constant gate capacitance to replace a supplied charge or Miller plateau model.','Gate charge can be voltage dependent and divided into distinct switching intervals. Bootstrap droop includes every named charge removal path.','Check coulombs = amperes × seconds and joules = volts × coulombs before multiplying by event rate.','A stronger driver can reduce overlap time but increases peak current and edge rate. More bootstrap capacitance reduces droop while raising refresh charge and area.','Supplied gate-charge and ramp models are reduced characterizations; they do not establish EMI, ringing or safe operating area.'),
 thermal:guide('Solve power and temperature as a feedback loop','Hot resistance and dissipated power must describe the same operating point.',[
 'Write the stated conduction-loss expression using RMS current, then connect temperature rise to power through thermal resistance.',
 'Substitute the temperature-dependent resistance law into that relation and solve for the self-consistent point, including the stability denominator.',
 'Check the resulting temperature and power against the law’s physical domain. If feedback gain approaches unity, report the model’s sensitivity rather than extrapolating a safe part rating.'
 ],'Use room-temperature RDS(on) after solving for a hot junction.','The resistance is the feedback element; changing temperature while holding it at its cold value breaks self-consistency.','Reinsert the calculated power into both the thermal-rise equation and the hot-resistance loss equation.','Lower thermal resistance and lower current reduce positive feedback. Heat sinking, device size and switching loss trade against cost and area.','The supplied linear thermal law omits transient thermal impedance and unnamed losses. Its stability threshold is not a datasheet safe-operating-area boundary.'),
 timing:guide('Put launch, data and capture on one timeline','Setup uses the late-data path; hold uses the early-data path and a different edge relationship.',[
 'Mark actual launch and capture edge arrival times, including the sign of skew. Write the data arrival relative to launch.',
 'For setup, compare maximum data delay with the capture edge minus setup and uncertainty. For hold, compare minimum data delay with the same-cycle capture hold window.',
 'Apply any multicycle or corner specification explicitly. Re-evaluate all competing paths after an optimization or added hold delay.'
 ],'Use the same delay corner or skew sign for both setup and hold.','Later capture often helps setup while making hold harder. A maximum path cannot stand in for a minimum-delay path.','Check a numerical timeline at the equality boundary; an earlier/later capture edge must change the deadline in the physically correct direction.','Adding delay can fix hold while reducing setup slack. Pipelining can raise throughput while adding latency and register/clock power.','This is a deterministic supplied timing model; unlisted jitter, on-chip variation and metastability are not established by its arithmetic.'),
 boolean:guide('Prove equivalence with discriminating input cases','A logic expression must match the entire allowed input domain.',[
 'Write the required output for each allowed combination or split the problem into cases on a select/control input.',
 'Derive product terms, a Shannon decomposition or a simpler algebraic identity that preserves all those cases.',
 'Test the rows where plausible candidate expressions differ, then exhaust the stated finite truth table where practical.'
 ],'Infer dynamic hazard freedom from a simplified truth table.','A truth table describes settled Boolean values. Unequal gate delays during a transition can still create glitches.','Compare all allowed settled input rows, and analyze changing-input paths separately if hazards are part of the task.','Fewer literals can improve readability, but physical area, delay and power depend on cell mapping, fanout and routing. A consensus term may deliberately add hardware to cover a hazard.','Only the specified 0/1 input domain is proved unless the problem explicitly covers X/Z states or illegal codes.'),
 arithmetic:guide('Separate the mathematical value from its stored bit pattern','Width, signedness, carry and overflow are distinct parts of the arithmetic contract.',[
 'Compute the exact integer result before truncation or saturation. Record operand width and signed/unsigned interpretation.',
 'Apply the specified storage rule: modulo wrapping discards upper bits, while saturation clamps at an endpoint.',
 'Compute carry, borrow, signed overflow or Gray conversion from its own definition and verify a boundary example that distinguishes the alternatives.'
 ],'Use the carry flag as signed overflow.','Unsigned carry measures an upper bit of the exact nonnegative sum. Signed overflow asks whether the exact signed result fits the destination range.','Use endpoint and sign-change examples, including a carry without signed overflow and signed overflow without carry when applicable.','Saturation can limit numerical excursions but costs detection and selection logic. Wider datapaths reduce quantization/wrap risk at an area and power cost.','The same bits can encode different values; interpretation and expression sizing follow the problem’s exact contract.'),
 throughput:guide('Count useful work and elapsed cycles separately','Peak clock rate alone does not determine payload throughput or latency.',[
 'Count payload units, framing/command overhead, stalls and pipeline fill explicitly under the supplied transfer rules.',
 'Divide useful completed work by the full elapsed time for throughput. Trace the first item separately when latency is requested.',
 'For a burst buffer or fractional enable, compute maximum occupancy or discrete spacing as well as the long-run mean.'
 ],'Use the average rate to guarantee every individual interval.','Bursts, stalls and integer tick quantization can create larger local gaps or occupancy peaks than a mean-rate calculation suggests.','Check one complete burst/period by a cycle-by-cycle count and keep bytes, bits, transfers and seconds distinct.','More buffering can absorb a stall at a storage and latency cost. Wider bursts amortize overhead but may increase blocking and response time.','The model’s handshake, simultaneous-event and off-by-one conventions define what counts in the interval.'),
 adc:guide('Apply the exact quantizer or endpoint convention','Resolution, code transition and linearity metrics use different reference constructions.',[
 'Write the number of levels and stated full-scale or endpoint definition before choosing a step size.',
 'Use the supplied rounding, floor, saturation and bit-order rule to obtain the code or error; keep code units distinct from volts.',
 'For noise, use the stated quantization model and integration bandwidth. For DNL/INL, compare the measured step or code with its specified ideal line.'
 ],'Use the endpoint span and 2^N divisor interchangeably without reading the convention.','An ideal code-bin ADC and an endpoint-fitted DAC can define their ideal increments differently. The question’s transfer rule decides.','Test zero, a code transition, and the upper endpoint under the exact stated rule.','More bits tighten settling and reference requirements; nominal resolution alone does not ensure accuracy or low noise. Reference drift can rescale every reading.','Uniform independent quantization noise is a model; coherent small signals or deterministic errors need not behave as white noise.'),
 cdc:guide('Separate synchronization probability from protocol correctness','A synchronizer’s reliability, latency and event-capture contract answer different questions.',[
 'Trace safe sample edges for the specified phase and pulse width before reasoning about destination latency or capture guarantees.',
 'For MTBF, use the supplied fitted constants and actual transition rate with consistent units. Add independent failure rates across crossings.',
 'Invert the exponential with a natural logarithm when solving a resolution-time target. Keep the statistical result separate from deterministic pulse delivery.'
 ],'Assume two registers guarantee capture of every pulse or make failure impossible.','A short pulse can fall between sample edges. Extra settling time reduces metastability escape probability but does not provide an event handshake.','Check the shortest allowed pulse phase and verify that system failure opportunities grow when more independent crossings are added.','More resolution time can improve fitted MTBF exponentially while increasing latency or consuming timing budget. Handshakes/toggles preserve event intent under their own protocol limits.','Hypothetical MTBF constants are not a named-device guarantee, and a mean interval is not a deterministic minimum lifetime.'),
 state:guide('Trace state and outputs on separate timelines','A next-state recovery rule does not automatically protect outputs between edges.',[
 'List the current state, external inputs and output rule before the active event. Decide whether the output is registered, Moore or Mealy.',
 'Apply transition priority and reset semantics only at the events that actually trigger the process.',
 'Inspect the complete interval until the next event, including illegal encodings and asynchronous input changes where the task permits them.'
 ],'Assume a safe next state makes the current illegal-state output safe immediately.','Recovery changes the register at an edge; combinational decoding can remain unsafe throughout the preceding interval.','Build a short state/input/event trace and calculate both current output and next state in each row.','One-hot encoding can simplify logic but uses more registers; illegal-state handling and output qualification may require extra gates or verification.','The transition table and output contract are authoritative. Storage width or recovery behavior cannot be inferred from a state name alone.'),
 hdlLogic:guide('Derive a truth table independently of the submitted expression','The workbench proves the known-bit Boolean contract supplied by the exercise.',[
 'Translate the requested output into a predicate over the named scalar inputs. For arithmetic flags, start from exact integer or bit-position definitions.',
 'Construct a different equivalent form using case splitting, product terms or generate/propagate logic. Respect the one-statement interface and allowed operators.',
 'Exhaust all allowed input patterns and compare the predicate with the statement. Include enable, mask, tie and boundary cases rather than only active examples.'
 ],'Submit syntactically plausible logic that omits a control or illegal-state case.','A mask, saved value or validity condition can change the entire output contract. Testing only the obvious asserted case misses those rows.','Check the reference predicate for every known-bit row; waveform timing and X/Z semantics need separate evidence.','Readable operators expose intent, while expanded expressions expose a derivation. Synthesis and layout determine the physical implementation and its timing.','This scalar workbench is a bounded combinational checker, not an external HDL simulator, synthesis result or proof of production timing.'),
 hdlEvents:guide('Separate expression evaluation from register updates','Nonblocking assignments evaluate using pre-update values on the triggering event.',[
 'Mark exactly which clock/reset/input events execute the process and note whether reset is synchronous or asynchronous.',
 'At an executing event, evaluate right-hand sides using the current values. Determine procedural priority and the last scheduled assignment to the same register.',
 'Commit scheduled nonblocking updates together, then use those new values for later events. For incomplete combinational assignments, identify the paths that retain storage.'
 ],'Propagate one nonblocking update immediately into a later right-hand side.','That turns concurrent register behavior into a software-style sequential update. Separate scheduling from commit to preserve HDL event semantics.','Use an event table with old values, scheduled assignments and committed values; include an event where the process does not execute.','Explicit default assignments improve combinational completeness. Intentional state needs clear reset and priority rules to make review and verification tractable.','Settled digital event semantics here exclude analog metastability, clock skew and propagation delay unless explicitly included.'),
 hdlSigned:guide('Resolve width and signedness before evaluating the operator','A signed declaration does not make every mixed expression signed.',[
 'Record the declared width and signedness of every operand, concatenation and cast at the operator in question.',
 'Apply the language’s stated sizing and signed/unsigned conversion before comparison, shifting or assignment truncation.',
 'Interpret the resulting bit pattern in the requested destination format and check a negative operand that distinguishes sign extension from zero extension.'
 ],'Assume an arithmetic operator or one signed operand forces a signed result everywhere.','Mixed expressions and concatenations can change interpretation before the final destination receives the bits. A later assignment cannot undo that earlier comparison.','Evaluate both the bit pattern and its integer interpretation; test the most negative or a negative-versus-positive boundary case.','Explicit casts and widths make intent reviewable and reduce portability mistakes. Wider intermediates can preserve information but require a deliberate truncation rule.','The question’s declared widths and supported language semantics matter; avoid relying on implicit unsized literal behavior.'),
 diagnosis:guide('Use a measurement that separates competing fault models','A plausible fault is useful only if it predicts the observed behavior.',[
 'Predict the nominal voltage, code or waveform under the stated model, then derive each proposed fault’s effect on the same measurement.',
 'Choose a discriminating measurement at a node or event where the hypotheses predict different results.',
 'Check whether the measurement itself loads the circuit or changes the fault state. Prefer the simplest hypothesis consistent with all supplied evidence.'
 ],'Choose a familiar fault without checking its predicted polarity or scale.','Several faults can sound plausible while predicting opposite rail, gain or timing behavior. Diagnostic evidence must distinguish them.','Recreate the observation from the selected fault model and identify one additional observation that could disprove it.','A high-impedance probe reduces disturbance but may add capacitance; instrument bandwidth, grounding and reference selection affect diagnosis.','The exercise’s enumerated fault hypotheses define its scope; a matching model is not proof that all real-world faults are excluded.'),
 opamp:guide('Close the feedback equation and then check limits','An ideal feedback gain is conditional on a valid linear operating point.',[
 'Write the summing-node or feedback-divider equation using the stated input current and polarity. Set input error to zero only for the ideal linear model.',
 'For finite open-loop gain, close vout = A(v+ − v−) explicitly. For a Schmitt circuit, solve the feedback node once for each pre-transition output state.',
 'Compare the requested output with rail headroom, bandwidth and slew constraints that the question actually supplies. Check the sign and limiting behavior.'
 ],'Apply a virtual short after the output has clipped or in a positive-feedback hysteresis state.','Negative feedback in the linear range supports a small input error. Saturation or state-dependent positive feedback needs a different operating condition.','Substitute the answer into both the feedback network and the amplifier relation; for a sine check the peak derivative against slew rate.','Closed-loop gain trades against bandwidth under a fixed gain-bandwidth model. Feedback resistor size also changes power, noise and input-bias error.','The op amp is ideal only to the extent stated. Common-mode range, output drive and stability cannot be inferred from a DC gain equation.'),
 converter:guide('Balance inductor voltage and capacitor charge over a cycle','A periodic converter operating point requires its storage states to repeat.',[
 'Separate the stated switching intervals and write each inductor voltage and capacitor current using the correct switch topology.',
 'Set mean inductor voltage to zero and mean capacitor current to zero in periodic steady state. Solve the duty or current relation under the stated conduction mode.',
 'Integrate the interval voltage to get current ripple, then check whether the minimum current remains compatible with the assumed mode.'
 ],'Use an ideal DC conversion ratio as proof of a valid ripple or conduction-mode assumption.','The ratio follows from a specified switching model; finite ripple can force a different mode at low load.','Check volt-seconds and charge per complete cycle with consistent peak-to-peak versus peak definitions.','Higher switching frequency can reduce ripple for fixed storage but adds switching and drive loss in real hardware. Larger inductance reduces ripple while changing size and transient response.','Ideal efficiency and fixed-duty equations exclude all unlisted switch drops, winding loss and control dynamics.'),
 interface:guide('Check both logic levels and the current or power budget','A digital interface still obeys analog voltage and current limits.',[
 'Compare worst specified driver high/low outputs with receiver input thresholds using the correct inequality direction.',
 'For capacitive switching power, use the stated activity definition and event rate; distinguish clock frequency from actual charging transitions.',
 'Report the weaker margin or limiting budget rather than selecting only the favorable logic level.'
 ],'Treat nominal logic-high voltage as the guaranteed high under every load.','Noise-margin guarantees use the supplied bounds. Switching activity also needs a convention; each charging event has a specific energy cost.','Check voltage-margin polarity and joules per event times events per second for power.','Lower supply can reduce CV² switching power but may shrink noise margin and speed. More buffering strengthens drive while adding power and load.','Use only the given output guarantees, thresholds, capacitance and activity model; no named logic-family compatibility is implied.'),
 coding:guide('Preserve the code construction and bit order','An error code is defined by its arithmetic convention and admitted error model.',[
 'Write the generator, check positions or address field widths exactly as stated. For a CRC use polynomial arithmetic over GF(2), where subtraction is XOR.',
 'Derive the check value or decoded field from those definitions before translating it to a decimal or packed bit pattern.',
 'Apply one explicit error or endpoint example and verify the resulting syndrome, recovery action or reconstructed address.'
 ],'Carry a familiar reflected-bit or parity convention into a differently defined code.','Bit order, augmentation, parity locations and address units change the result even when the high-level algorithm has the same name.','Reconstruct the complete codeword or byte address from the reported fields and verify the defining check equation.','Extra parity and alignment structure trade storage or bandwidth for detection, correction or simpler access. Coverage depends on the exact admitted errors.','A nonzero syndrome detects an inconsistency under this code; it does not automatically locate an arbitrary error or guarantee correction outside the stated model.')
,
 series:guide('Check the answer through voltage division','Every element in an unbranched series loop carries the same current.',[
 'Sum the series resistances and compute the common current from the source voltage.',
 'Independently divide the source voltage in proportion to each resistance, then recover current from either individual resistor voltage.',
 'Add the resistor voltage drops to close KVL and compare total resistor dissipation with source power.'
 ],'Give the larger series resistor a smaller current.','A junction with no extra branch cannot accumulate charge in DC steady state; current is common while voltage drops differ.','Each resistor must give the same V/R current and all signed drops must sum to the source voltage.','More total series resistance reduces current and power at fixed supply. The distribution of resistance determines individual voltage and heating, not different series currents.','This reasoning assumes the stated ideal source, unbranched topology and DC resistors.'),
 bjtCurrent:guide('Audit all three terminal currents','Finite beta relates collectors and bases; emitters carry their sum.',[
 'Use the forward-active definition IC = βIB and the terminal identity IE = IC + IB = (β + 1)IB.',
 'For a compound connection, determine exactly which first-device terminal drives the next device. A Darlington connects the first emitter current to the second base current.',
 'Reconstruct the external supply or load current as the sum of the physical connected terminal currents, then verify the stated operating-region assumptions.'
 ],'Apply a product of betas to the wrong external current.','Compound-device collector current can include multiple collector branches, while an internal drive path may carry an emitter current with a beta-plus-one factor.','Check KCL at every joined terminal rather than treating a compound device as one unexplained beta.','Higher current gain reduces required driver current under the model, but device spread, temperature, compliance and stored charge still constrain a real bias or switch.','Forward-active beta is conditional; it cannot force a collector load beyond its supply-limited current.'),
 junction:guide('Use the incremental or ratio form of the junction law','An exponential current law is simplest when ratios or derivatives remove the unknown scale.',[
 'For two currents at the same temperature, divide their exponential laws so the shared saturation-current scale cancels.',
 'For a small-signal conductance, differentiate the current with respect to the controlling junction voltage at the stated bias.',
 'For a diode-connected device, sum every terminal-current contribution at the shorted input node; finite base current or output slope can add conductance.'
 ],'Replace a diode-connected input with only its collector-current derivative.','The external test current can include both base and collector increments. The supplied finite-beta and output-resistance terms decide the total.','Check siemens = amperes per volt and ohms as the reciprocal; for a current ratio check that its natural logarithm is dimensionless.','Exponential junction sensitivity enables compact ratio and temperature circuits, while bias-current variation and device matching affect accuracy. Lower dynamic resistance generally costs more standing current.','Use the supplied ideality, thermal voltage and output-slope assumptions; a local derivative is not a large-signal constant resistance.'),
 followerLimits:guide('Find the conducting window before applying a gain','A follower can cut off or lose collector headroom even when its input keeps moving.',[
 'Use the stated junction drop and the actual load path to derive the conducting output relation for each polarity or transistor.',
 'Solve the lower-current/cutoff and upper-headroom boundaries independently. For complementary devices, identify the interval in which neither junction is forward biased.',
 'Reconstruct the terminal voltages at each boundary and check the output definition or signed dead-zone endpoints requested.'
 ],'Extend a linear follower relation past cutoff or into an impossible collector voltage.','The relation assumes conduction in the named region. At a boundary the circuit changes branch and needs its corresponding piecewise model.','Evaluate the junction and collector-base conditions on both sides of each proposed transition.','Biasing a complementary follower can reduce crossover distortion but introduces quiescent current and thermal design requirements. More supply voltage buys headroom at a power cost.','The hard junction drops and ideal loads are supplied exercise approximations; real crossover and clipping transitions are gradual and device dependent.'),
 bodyDiode:guide('Trace the intrinsic diode independently of the channel','An off gate does not remove a MOSFET’s intrinsic reverse-current path.',[
 'Identify physical source, drain and body connections from the stated device orientation rather than relabeling terminals to match an instantaneous current.',
 'Mark the intrinsic body-diode anode and cathode, then compare the applied polarity with its supplied forward-drop model.',
 'If it conducts, solve the complete source-resistor-diode loop and compute dissipated power from the actual diode current and drop.'
 ],'Conclude zero current solely because VGS is below threshold.','That only addresses channel conduction in the stated model. The intrinsic body diode has a separate polarity and can conduct with an off gate.','KVL must include the forward diode drop and all series resistance; reverse polarity must fail that forward-conduction test.','Back-to-back devices can block both polarities under an appropriate drive scheme, adding resistance, gate-drive complexity and component count. A single off MOSFET is not an ideal bidirectional open switch.','Use the fixed diode drop and channel assumptions supplied here; reverse recovery, avalanche and device safe operating area are separate effects.'),
 storageWidth:guide('Count distinguishable states before counting bits','Binary storage capacity grows by a factor of two per bit.',[
 'Count the exact number of states or distinct codes required, including whether the terminal count is inclusive.',
 'For a dense binary register choose the smallest integer n for which 2^n covers that count; compare the capacity of n − 1 to prove minimality.',
 'For one-hot encoding count one storage bit per state, then distinguish register count from the complexity of decoding and illegal-state detection.'
 ],'Round a base-two logarithm down when the requirement is not a power of two.','A smaller register cannot represent every required distinguishable state. Unused binary codes are normal and may need explicit recovery logic.','Verify both 2^(n − 1) < required states and 2^n ≥ required states where n is positive.','Dense encoding saves registers; one-hot can simplify state decoding at a register and clock-power cost. Physical area or speed cannot be inferred from bit count alone.','Storage counts concern the specified states and encoding only; reset, parity and additional control registers are excluded unless stated.'),
 address:guide('Reconstruct the byte address from its fields','Address bits select physical units in the order defined by the memory organization.',[
 'Separate byte offset within a word from the word index before selecting an interleaved bank.',
 'Apply the stated bank mapping to the word index, then remove those bank-select bits to obtain the row inside that bank.',
 'Reconstruct word index from row and bank and finally add the byte offset. It must reproduce the original byte address exactly.'
 ],'Take bank-select bits directly from the lowest byte-address bits.','Those low bits may select a byte within a multi-byte word. Skipping the offset changes the physical bank for the same byte address.','Check address = bytes-per-word × (banks × row + bank) + byte-offset for the stated low-order interleaving.','Interleaving can distribute consecutive words across banks but does not guarantee conflict-free arbitrary accesses. Word size and alignment affect available parallelism.','This mapping assumes the exact bank interleaving and address unit supplied; caches, virtual-address translation and burst policies are separate systems.')

};
